ns incomplete.A growing body of evidence suggests that large-scale, rhythmic electrical oscillations -spanning multiple brain regions and frequency bands -serve as a fundamental organizational substrate for cognition.These global oscillations coordinate the temporal dynamics of neural firing, gate synaptic plasticity, facilitate long-range communication, and support the binding of distributed representations into coherent mental states.This paper synthesizes current research on the role of theta, gamma, alpha, beta, and delta oscillations in episodic memory, working memory, attention, and higher-order cognition.We argue that a comprehensive theory of memory must move beyond static network topology to embrace the dynamic, rhythmic, and global nature of brain activity.We further discuss how oscillatory dysfunction underlies major cognitive disorders and how entrainment-based therapies ma

